One way to implement a Java program that applies English language grammar rules to correct a sentence is to use a natural language processing (NLP) library such as Stanford CoreNLP or Apache OpenNLP. These libraries provide various tools and models for parsing and analyzing text, including part-of-speech tagging, dependency parsing, and named entity recognition.
Here's an example code snippet using Stanford CoreNLP to correct the sentence "The cat eating the fish":
```java
import edu.stanford.nlp.pipeline.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.ling.*;
import edu.stanford.nlp.util.*;
public class SentenceCorrector {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // create a StanfordCoreNLP object with the annotators we need
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.setProperty("annotators", "tokenize, ssplit, pos, parse");
        StanfordCoreNLP pipeline = new StanfordCoreNLP(props);
        // annotate the sentence
        String sentence = "The cat eating the fish.";
        Annotation annotation = new Annotation(sentence);
        pipeline.annotate(annotation);
        // get the parse tree
        Tree tree = annotation.get(CoreAnnotations.SentencesAnnotation.class).get(0).get(TreeCoreAnnotations.TreeAnnotation.class);
        // correct the sentence by applying grammar rules
        String correctedSentence = correctSentence(tree);
        // print the corrected sentence
        System.out.println(correctedSentence);
    }
    private static String correctSentence(Tree tree) {
        // apply grammar rules to correct the sentence
        // in this example, we simply replace the VP (verb phrase) with a corrected version
        Tree vp = tree.getLeaves().get(1).ancestor(2, tree);
        vp.setLabel("VP");
        vp.setChildren(new ArrayList<Tree>(Arrays.asList(
            new LabeledScoredTreeNode(new CoreLabel().setWord("eats").setTag("VBZ")),
            vp.getChild(1)
        )));
        // return the corrected sentence
        return tree.toString();
    }
}
```
In this example, we first create a `StanfordCoreNLP` object with the annotators we need (tokenize, sentence split, part-of-speech tagging, and parsing). We then annotate the input sentence using this pipeline and get the parse tree for the sentence.
We then define a `correctSentence` method that applies grammar rules to correct the sentence. In this example, we simply replace the verb phrase (VP) "eating the fish" with the corrected version "eats the fish". 
Finally, we print the corrected sentence by calling `correctSentence` on the parse tree and converting it back to a string. The output of this program should be "The cat eats the fish."

