To implement a binary search algorithm to find the lowest value in an array of integers, you can follow these steps:
1. Sort the array in ascending order using a sorting algorithm like quicksort or mergesort.
2. Perform a binary search on the sorted array to find the lowest value.
Here's the updated Java code:
```
import java.util.Arrays;
public class BinarySearchExample {
   public static void main(String[] args) {
      int[] arr = {5, 3, 7, 1, 9};
      Arrays.sort(arr); // Step 1: Sort the array
      int min = arr[0];
      int left = 0;
      int right = arr.length - 1;
      while (left <= right) { // Step 2: Perform binary search
         int mid = (left + right) / 2;
         if (arr[mid] < min) {
            min = arr[mid];
         }
         if (arr[mid] < arr[left]) {
            right = mid - 1;
         } else {
            left = mid + 1;
         }
      }
      System.out.println("The lowest value in the array is: " + min);
   }
}
```
In this updated code, we first sort the array using the Arrays.sort() method. Then, we initialize the variables left and right to the first and last indices of the array, respectively. We perform a binary search by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half until we find the lowest value. At each iteration, we update the min variable if the middle element is smaller than the current minimum. We also update the left or right variable depending on whether the middle element is smaller or larger than the leftmost element. Finally, we print the lowest value found.

